        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Shop Vac Losing Suction",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/shop-vac-losing-suction/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why is my shop vac losing suction?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Nine times out of ten it is a blinded filter, particularly if the loss has been gradual and the machine has been used on fine dust. Take the cartridge out, tap it clean against a hard surface, and refit. If the loss was sudden, check for a blockage in the hose and a full or split collection bag instead."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I find a blockage in a shop vac hose?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Disconnect the hose, stretch it straight, and look through it toward a light \u2014 a clear hose shows daylight. If not, feed a garden hose, drain auger or straightened coat hanger through from each end. Most blockages sit within a couple of feet of an end and are usually a rag, tape, plastic sheeting or damp sawdust."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why does my shop vac have suction at the machine but not the hose?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "That split \u2014 strong at the bare inlet port, weak with the hose fitted \u2014 means the restriction is downstream: the hose, an extension wand, or the nozzle. Check the joint between wand sections and the intake slot on the floor brush, which collects a felt of hair and fibre that blocks airflow completely."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "My shop vac stopped suddenly while vacuuming water. Is it broken?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Almost certainly not. The float shut-off has risen with the water level and sealed the intake to keep liquid away from the motor, which is exactly what it is designed to do. Empty the tank and it will resume. If it does not, check that the float has dropped and is not stuck on debris around its cage."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a shop vac lose suction from an air leak?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, and it is the cause nobody checks. A worn hose cuff, a perished tank gasket, a latch that no longer clamps, or a split in the hose corrugation all let air into the system that never reaches the nozzle. Run the machine and hold a strip of tissue near the lid joint \u2014 it will flutter at the leak."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will cleaning a shop vac filter restore suction?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually, and dramatically. A cartridge caked with fine dust can lose half the machine's airflow while looking only mildly dirty. Tapping it against a hard surface outside recovers most of that. Once cleaning no longer restores airflow, or the pleats are torn or permanently grey, the filter is finished and needs replacing."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a weak shop vac worth repairing?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Filters, hoses, cuffs, gaskets and motor brushes are all cheap and worth replacing on any machine. A failed motor or damaged impeller is usually not worth repairing on a budget vac, since parts and time approach the price of a new one, but is a reasonable repair on a mid-range or professional machine with real parts support."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
