    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Suction does not disappear from a shop vac; it leaks away through a loaded filter, a blocked hose, or a seal that no longer seals. The motor is the last thing to suspect and the first thing everybody blames. Work through it in this order and most machines are back to full strength in ten minutes.
        </p>

        <div id="start-here" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Start Here: It Is Almost Always the Filter
          </h2>

          <p>
            A shop vac that has lost suction feels like a failing motor and virtually never is. The motor either runs or it does not; what degrades gradually is the air path, and the most restrictive point in that path is the filter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before opening anything else, take the cartridge out and look at it in daylight. A filter that has been used on fine dust develops a grey cake across the pleats that can halve airflow while still looking, at a glance, merely dusty. Tap it firmly against a hard surface outside, refit it, and try the machine again. In our experience this alone resolves the majority of sudden suction complaints.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two related checks take another minute. Is a collection bag fitted, and is it full or split? A bag that has burst dumps its contents against the cartridge and blinds it immediately. And is the filter damp? A cartridge refitted after wet work without drying fully is effectively a solid disc as far as airflow is concerned.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Sixty-Second Diagnostic</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Pull the hose off and put your hand over the inlet port on the machine itself.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the suction at the bare port is strong, the problem is in the hose, the wand or the nozzle. If it is weak at the port too, the problem is inside the machine: filter, tank seal, or a blocked impeller. This single test splits the problem in half before you take anything apart.</p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div id="hose-blockages" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Blockages in the Hose, Wand and Nozzle
          </h2>

          <p>
            If the bare port pulls hard and the hose does not, something is stuck. Shop vac hoses hide blockages well because the corrugations trap debris part-way along and the machine still makes its usual noise.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Finding the Blockage</h3>
          <p>
            Disconnect the hose completely and stretch it out straight on the floor. Look through it toward a light — a clear hose shows daylight. If not, feed a length of garden hose, a drain auger, or a straightened wire coat hanger through from each end. Most blockages are within a couple of feet of one end, usually a rag, a wad of packing tape, a plastic bag or a clump of damp sawdust.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Wand and Nozzle</h3>
          <p>
            Extension wands are the most common hiding place, particularly where two sections join, and floor brushes collect a felt of hair and fibre across the intake slot that no amount of suction clears. Take the head off and check the slot itself, not just the tube behind it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Prevention is largely about what goes in. Long strips of plastic sheeting, socks, and offcuts of underlay wrap themselves into a plug at the first bend. On renovation work, pick up the large stuff by hand first — it takes less time than clearing a hose.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="leaks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Air Leaks: The Cause Nobody Checks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every cubic foot of air entering the system somewhere other than the nozzle is airflow you do not get where you need it. Leaks produce exactly the symptom people describe as 'it just is not as strong as it used to be', without any dramatic failure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the hose cuff at the machine. Shop vac cuffs wear and go loose, and a cuff that can be rotated freely with no resistance is usually leaking. A wrap of electrical tape is a legitimate field fix; a replacement cuff is the proper one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next check the tank gasket and the latches. The lid assembly seals against the drum rim, and a perished gasket, a warped rim from a knock, or a latch that no longer clamps fully lets air in all the way around. Hold a strip of tissue near the joint with the machine running — it will flutter at the leak.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally check the hose itself for splits, particularly at the ends where it flexes most. A hose that has been dragged around a garage for years develops cracks in the corrugation that are invisible until you bend the section and look.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="full-tank" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. A Full Tank, a Tripped Float, and Wet Debris
          </h2>

          <p>
            On wet pickup, the float shut-off rises with the water level and seals the intake to stop liquid reaching the motor. When it does its job, the machine's note changes and suction stops almost entirely — which is precisely what it is meant to do, and what a lot of people interpret as a breakdown.
          </p>

          <p>
            Empty the tank and the machine returns to normal. If suction does not return after emptying, check that the float has dropped back down and is not stuck on debris or a dried deposit around its cage. Floats stick, particularly after picking up anything other than clean water.
          </p>

          <p>
            On dry work, a genuinely full tank restricts the air volume the machine can pull through, and packed damp sawdust does it long before the tank looks full. Sawdust that went in wet compacts into a dense mass that is effectively a second filter, and clearing it restores the machine immediately.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more wet-work specific: if the vac has been used on liquid and then goes weak on dry pickup, look for standing water in the hose. A hose left coiled with water in the low points has a plug of liquid in it that blocks airflow entirely, and it will not clear itself until the hose is stretched out and drained.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="impeller" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. When It Is Actually the Machine
          </h2>

          <p>
            Having cleared the filter, the hose and the leaks, a short list of mechanical causes remains. These are far less common but worth knowing.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Blocked or Damaged Impeller</h3>
          <p>
            Debris that gets past a missing filter can reach the impeller and either jam it or chip the blades. The symptom is a change in pitch — a rattle, a whine, or a vibration that was not there before — alongside weak suction. Unplug the machine, remove the motor head, and inspect. A chipped impeller needs replacing, not straightening.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Motor Brush Wear</h3>
          <p>
            Universal motors use carbon brushes that wear down over years. Worn brushes produce a machine that runs but is noticeably weaker, sometimes with visible sparking around the motor vents. On many shop vacs the brushes are replaceable for very little money, and doing so restores full performance.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bearing Failure</h3>
          <p>
            A motor that has ingested fine dust — because somebody ran it without a filter — gradually loses bearing life. The sound is a dry rumble or a rising whine, and the honest answer at this point is usually a new machine, since the labour and parts to rebuild rarely make sense on a domestic vac.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is also the simple case of an undersized extension cord. A long, thin cord drops voltage at the motor, and the machine genuinely runs weaker. Try it plugged straight into the wall before condemning anything.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="prevention" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Keeping Suction Where It Should Be
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost all of this is preventable with three habits that cost minutes a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fit a collection bag for any fine dust work. It keeps the cartridge clean, which is where suction actually goes, and it makes emptying a sealed operation instead of a dust cloud.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tap the cartridge out between sessions rather than when the machine has already gone weak. Airflow falls gradually, so you adapt to it without noticing; a filter cleaned on a schedule keeps the machine feeling new.
          </p>

          <p>
            Empty the tank after each job rather than storing debris in it. Damp material compacts, smells, and holds moisture against the filter base. Store the vac dry, with the lid unlatched if it has been wet.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Symptom</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Most Likely Cause</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">First Fix</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gradual loss over weeks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Blinded filter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tap or replace the cartridge</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sudden loss mid-job</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Blockage or full bag</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check hose, then bag</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Weak at the hose, strong at the port</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hose or nozzle blockage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stretch hose, look through it</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Weak everywhere, high pitch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tank leak or loose lid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check gasket and latches</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stops suddenly on wet work</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Float shut-off triggered</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Empty the tank</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rattle or whine plus weakness</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Impeller damage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unplug and inspect motor head</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div id="when-to-replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When to Repair and When to Replace
          </h2>

          <p>
            Shop vacs are cheap enough that repair economics are blunt. Filters, hoses, cuffs, gaskets and even motor brushes are worth replacing — all of them are inexpensive, and the machine is otherwise simple and long-lived.
          </p>

          <p>
            A failed motor or a damaged impeller on a budget machine generally is not worth repairing, because the parts and time approach the cost of a new vac. On a mid-range or professional machine with a genuine parts supply, both are reasonable repairs and the machine can run another decade.
          </p>

          <p>
            The clearest signal that a machine is finished is a motor that smells hot, sparks visibly at the vents, or has developed a dry rumble in the bearings. That is almost always the result of dust ingestion from running filterless, and it is not recoverable in a domestic unit.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are replacing, this is the moment to fix the mismatch that caused the problem in the first place. A machine with easy filter access, a bag option, a 2.5-inch hose and a drain port makes every one of the habits above easier to keep — our <a href="/best-shop-vac/">shop vac buyer's guide</a> compares models on exactly those practical points rather than on horsepower stickers.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the Motor Has Failed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Motors either run or they do not; what degrades gradually is the air path. A machine that has 'lost power' over weeks almost always has a loaded filter, and one that lost it suddenly has a blockage or a full bag. Check those before concluding anything about the motor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cleaning the Filter Only When Suction Is Already Gone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Airflow falls gradually enough that you adapt without noticing, so by the time the machine feels weak, it has been underperforming for weeks. Tap the cartridge out between sessions on a schedule and the vac stays at full strength.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Hose Cuff</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cuff that spins freely on the port with no resistance is leaking, and the airflow lost there never reaches the nozzle. It is a two-minute fix with tape or a replacement cuff and it is one of the most common causes of a machine that is 'not as strong as it used to be'.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Vacuuming Long Plastic and Fabric Without Thinking</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sheeting, underlay offcuts, rags and socks wrap into a plug at the first bend in the hose and stop the machine completely. On renovation work, pick the large, stringy material up by hand first — it takes far less time than clearing a blocked hose.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing the Vac With Debris in the Tank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Damp sawdust and leaves compact into a dense mass that restricts airflow, hold moisture against the filter base, and turn sour within days. Empty the tank after each job and leave the lid unlatched after wet work so the inside dries.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my shop vac losing suction?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Nine times out of ten it is a blinded filter, particularly if the loss has been gradual and the machine has been used on fine dust. Take the cartridge out, tap it clean against a hard surface, and refit. If the loss was sudden, check for a blockage in the hose and a full or split collection bag instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I find a blockage in a shop vac hose?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Disconnect the hose, stretch it straight, and look through it toward a light — a clear hose shows daylight. If not, feed a garden hose, drain auger or straightened coat hanger through from each end. Most blockages sit within a couple of feet of an end and are usually a rag, tape, plastic sheeting or damp sawdust.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my shop vac have suction at the machine but not the hose?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                That split — strong at the bare inlet port, weak with the hose fitted — means the restriction is downstream: the hose, an extension wand, or the nozzle. Check the joint between wand sections and the intake slot on the floor brush, which collects a felt of hair and fibre that blocks airflow completely.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">My shop vac stopped suddenly while vacuuming water. Is it broken?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost certainly not. The float shut-off has risen with the water level and sealed the intake to keep liquid away from the motor, which is exactly what it is designed to do. Empty the tank and it will resume. If it does not, check that the float has dropped and is not stuck on debris around its cage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a shop vac lose suction from an air leak?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is the cause nobody checks. A worn hose cuff, a perished tank gasket, a latch that no longer clamps, or a split in the hose corrugation all let air into the system that never reaches the nozzle. Run the machine and hold a strip of tissue near the lid joint — it will flutter at the leak.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will cleaning a shop vac filter restore suction?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, and dramatically. A cartridge caked with fine dust can lose half the machine's airflow while looking only mildly dirty. Tapping it against a hard surface outside recovers most of that. Once cleaning no longer restores airflow, or the pleats are torn or permanently grey, the filter is finished and needs replacing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a weak shop vac worth repairing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Filters, hoses, cuffs, gaskets and motor brushes are all cheap and worth replacing on any machine. A failed motor or damaged impeller is usually not worth repairing on a budget vac, since parts and time approach the price of a new one, but is a reasonable repair on a mid-range or professional machine with real parts support.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

